			<div class="content" id="post-content-3571666">
									

<p>I think this definitely deserves more discussion than it's been getting&#8212;it was pretty clear, reading this, that you had put a ton of effort into the work. I neutral voted this, partly because I didn't 100% get it when I read it, partly because some parts didn't work super well for me.</p>
<p>In terms of not getting it, this is the rare case when someone crosslinks a bunch and I actually get most of it, because I'm caught up with Fifthism. That said, I flat-out did not understand what relationship the anafabula had with this, and I thought you were referring to it in a more roundabout sense (like all the other crosslinks in the article, such as 3512) as opposed to making it a major plot point. Specifically, I didn't really see how a narrative that could trigger the anafabula was involved, and while I'm sure that if I sat down and talked things out with AE he could explain it and I'd be convinced, I didn't get it while reading the article.</p>
<p>The thing about trippy weird shit like this, though, is that I don't think I have to get it to enjoy it. Having come off being a site reader for several years, I didn't even understand that <a href="/scp-3005">SCP=3005</a> was about Fifthism and I still immensely enjoyed it. When it comes to this article, the imagery and voice of the subject losing themselves to Fifthism is really important, and what didn't work for me is that half the presentation of this imagery seems wrapped up in a bunch of clever memetic/Fifthist play, which is hard enough to follow even when presented in plain language. To go back to the example of 3005, that introduces a few concrete, central images and then corrupts the shit out of them; I could never grasp onto this article firmly enough to understand the starting point of the corruption.</p>

